  "title": "Self-Storage Facility Engineering | Structural & MEP Design | Apex Grid Engineering",
  "description": "Self-storage engineering for developers: structural, MEP, and civil design for climate-controlled and drive-up facilities. 49-state licensed. 2-week turnarounds for multi-site rollouts.",
  "h1": "Self-Storage Facility Engineering",
  "kicker": "Self-Storage · Structural + MEP + Civil",
  "answer": "Apex Grid Engineering designs self-storage facilities for developers who build on speed: structural, mechanical, electrical, plumbing, and civil engineering for climate-controlled and drive-up storage, from single-site ground-up projects to multi-state prototype rollouts. As a veteran-owned firm licensed in 49 states plus DC with 10+ licensed professional engineers, we deliver permit-ready drawing packages on a two-week turnaround — the tempo storage development demands.",
  "sections": [
    {"heading": "Why self-storage developers choose Apex Grid", "body": "Self-storage is a speed business. A developer who ties up a site in January needs permit drawings in weeks — not the quarters that traditional engineering firms quote — because every month of delay is a month of lost lease-up. Apex Grid Engineering exists for exactly this tempo: an integrated MEP-plus-structural team under one roof, licensed in 49 states plus the District of Columbia, that delivers permit-ready self-storage drawing packages on a two-week turnaround once the basis of design is set. When a developer is rolling out five, ten, twenty facilities across multiple states, they do not need twenty engineers of record — they need one accountable team that already knows how storage buildings go together."},
    {"heading": "The engineering inside a storage building", "body": "A self-storage facility looks simple until you engineer one. The International Building Code classifies self-service storage (mini-storage) as Group S-1 moderate-hazard storage occupancy under IBC Section 311.2 — a classification that drives the fire protection strategy, allowable heights and areas, and egress design. Most jurisdictions require automatic sprinkler protection per NFPA 13 once the fire area crosses the IBC thresholds, and the sprinkler design has to account for compartmentalized units with limited access. Structural design is typically a pre-engineered metal building (PEMB) system or conventional steel: long clear spans for drive aisles, wind and seismic design per ASCE 7, and roof systems detailed for the equipment they carry. The MEP package covers climate-control HVAC for the conditioned buildings, electrical for lighting, gate operators, access control, and camera rough-in, plus plumbing for the leasing office and site utilities."},
    {"heading": "Climate-controlled storage: the HVAC engineering that protects the product", "body": "Climate control is what separates premium storage from commodity storage — and it is an engineering discipline, not a thermostat setting. The industry standard targets unit temperatures roughly between 55 and 85 degrees Fahrenheit with relative humidity held below 60 percent to prevent mold and mildew. Achieving that across tens of thousands of square feet of compartmentalized space takes deliberate design: split-system HVAC units on wide set-point ranges for long runtimes that wring out moisture, dedicated dehumidification in humid climates, and careful air distribution so no dead zone drifts out of range. Storage HVAC is sized at roughly 1,250 to 1,600 square feet per ton — far leaner than office or residential — because the goods themselves displace air volume and the set-point band is wide. Undersize the dehumidification and you get mold claims; oversize the cooling and you get short-cycling, high humidity, and wasted capital. Our mechanical engineers design for the humidity number first and the temperature number second."},
    {"heading": "Site planning: unit mix, drive aisles, and the geometry of revenue", "body": "A storage facility's revenue is drawn in plan view before a single footing is poured. Unit mix — the blend of 5x5s through 10x30s, climate-controlled versus drive-up, plus RV and boat storage — has to match the submarket's demographics, and the site plan has to make every unit rentable: drive aisles wide enough for box trucks and trailer turning radii, one-way circulation where it reduces conflicts, gate and keypad placement that keeps queues off the street, and stormwater detention that satisfies the municipality without eating the rentable footprint. Fire access lanes, hydrant coverage, and the fire department's turning requirements constrain the layout as hard as any zoning setback. We engineer the civil, structural, and MEP packages together so the site plan that wins the pro forma is the site plan that passes plan check."},
    {"heading": "Built for the multi-site rollout", "body": "The best storage developers do not build one facility — they build a program: a prototype design adapted across sites, states, and AHJs. That is where a 49-state licensed firm earns its keep. We take a developer's prototype and produce jurisdiction-ready drawing sets for each site: same unit mix logic and brand standard, re-engineered for local wind speeds, seismic design categories, snow loads, energy code editions, and the local building official's amendments. One point of responsibility, one coordinated package, permit-ready on a two-week cycle. Developers doing multi-site rollouts do not have time for an engineer who is learning storage on their project — we have the building type in our bones."}
  ],
  "faqs": [
    {"question": "How fast can Apex Grid deliver self-storage engineering?", "answer": "Two weeks for a permit-ready drawing package once the basis of design is complete — site plan, unit mix, and building type confirmed. That turnaround is the reason storage developers hire us: the integrated MEP-plus-structural team eliminates the inter-discipline coordination delays that stretch other firms' schedules into months. Multi-site rollouts get a program cadence: prototype once, then jurisdiction-adapted sets on a rolling schedule."},
    {"question": "What does self-storage engineering cost?", "answer": "Engineering fees scale with scope — single-story drive-up versus multi-story climate-controlled, ground-up versus conversion — but developers should budget engineering as a fraction of a project whose hard costs run roughly $50 to $120 per square foot nationally. The expensive mistakes are not the fee; they are the redesign loops when the structural, MEP, and civil packages were never coordinated. One integrated team removes that risk."},
    {"question": "Do you design climate-controlled facilities?", "answer": "Yes — it is the majority of what we design. Climate-controlled storage (roughly 55-85°F, humidity below 60%) requires deliberate HVAC engineering: wide-setpoint split systems, dedicated dehumidification, and air distribution designed for compartmentalized units. We size for the humidity target first, which is where most storage HVAC designs fail."},
    {"question": "Can you handle projects in multiple states?", "answer": "That is the core of our practice. Apex Grid Engineering is licensed in 49 states — every U.S. state except Alaska — plus the District of Columbia. For a developer rolling out a prototype across state lines, we adapt the design to each jurisdiction's wind, seismic, snow, energy, and fire code requirements under a single point of responsibility."},
    {"question": "Do you stamp drawings from other engineers?", "answer": "Only after responsible-charge review. A licensed engineer of record must review the design basis, verify or re-perform the engineering, and confirm the documents are eligible for sealing. Unverifiable drawings are not sealed — that protects the developer's project and our licenses."}
  ],
