    "answer": "Apex Grid Engineering designs self-storage facilities across Idaho — structural, mechanical, electrical, plumbing, and civil engineering for climate-controlled and drive-up projects, delivered on a two-week permit cycle for developers building at speed. Boise is one of the West's fastest-growing metros; in-migration from coastal states has outpaced storage supply in the Treasure Valley",
    "sections": [
    {"heading": "Why developers build self-storage in Idaho", "body": "Boise is one of the West's fastest-growing metros; in-migration from coastal states has outpaced storage supply in the Treasure Valley. Idaho ranked among the fastest-growing states by percentage for most of the 2020s — new arrivals rent storage while they house-hunt. Developers follow rooftops and renter turnover, and Idaho delivers both — which is why the Treasure Valley (Boise-Nampa-Meridian) keeps drawing new projects even as national deliveries moderate. For an engineering partner, that means volume: prototype designs adapted across the state's metros, each one needing jurisdiction-ready drawings on a developer's schedule, not an engineer's."},
    {"heading": "Self-storage MEP engineering for Idaho projects", "body": "The MEP package for a Idaho storage facility starts with the climate: semi-arid continental — hot summers and cold winters demand full four-season HVAC design. Climate-controlled buildings target roughly 55 to 85 degrees Fahrenheit with relative humidity below 60 percent, and the HVAC design — typically wide-setpoint split systems sized around 1,250 to 1,600 square feet per ton with dedicated dehumidification — is engineered for the humidity number first. Electrical covers LED site and unit lighting, gate operators, keypad access control, camera and security rough-in, and the leasing office. Plumbing serves the office restrooms and site hose bibbs; fire alarm and sprinkler monitoring tie into the access system. Every system is sealed by a Idaho-licensed PE under responsible charge."},
    {"heading": "Structural engineering for Idaho storage buildings", "body": "Most Idaho storage projects use pre-engineered metal building (PEMB) systems or conventional steel framing: economical long spans, fast erection, and a structure the MEP trades can actually build around. Structural design per ASCE 7 accounts for the jurisdiction's wind speed and exposure, seismic design category, and — where applicable — snow and frost depth for foundations. Multi-story climate-controlled buildings add floor vibration, elevator shafts, and heavier cumulative loads; single-story drive-up product lives or dies on slab and drainage detailing. Because our structural and MEP teams share the project, equipment weights, curb details, and penetration layouts are coordinated before permit — not discovered at plan check."},
    {"heading": "Code and permitting for storage in Idaho", "body": "Self-service storage is classified as Group S-1 moderate-hazard storage occupancy under IBC Section 311.2, which sets the framework for fire protection, allowable area, and egress. Automatic sprinkler protection per NFPA 13 is required once the fire area crosses IBC thresholds — a line most commercial-scale facilities cross — and the compartmentalized unit layout shapes the sprinkler and fire alarm design. Idaho enforces IBC-based codes statewide On the civil side, drive-aisle geometry, fire apparatus access, and stormwater detention are the plan-check items that stall unprepared submittals. We submit coordinated packages that answer the reviewer's questions before they are asked."},
    {"heading": "The two-week turnaround for Idaho developers", "body": "Storage developers doing multi-site rollouts cannot wait a quarter for drawings. Our prototype-to-permit workflow delivers jurisdiction-ready drawing sets on a two-week cycle once the basis of design is set: the developer's unit mix and brand standard, re-engineered for Idaho wind, seismic, snow, energy, and fire code requirements. One team, one point of responsibility, drawings that keep the construction schedule intact."}
    ],
    "faqs": [
    {"question": "How much does self-storage engineering cost in Idaho?", "answer": "Engineering fees scale with building type — single-story drive-up versus multi-story climate-controlled, ground-up versus conversion. Nationally, storage hard costs run roughly $50 to $120 per square foot, and engineering is a small fraction of that. The costly failures are redesign loops from uncoordinated disciplines, which an integrated MEP-plus-structural team avoids by design."},
    {"question": "How fast can you deliver permit drawings in Idaho?", "answer": "Two weeks from a complete basis of design. Idaho projects move through our prototype-to-permit workflow: confirm the site plan, unit mix, and building type, and the coordinated structural/MEP/civil package follows on a fixed cadence built for developers doing multi-site rollouts."},
    {"question": "Do Idaho storage facilities need sprinklers?", "answer": "In most cases, yes. Self-storage is Group S-1 occupancy, and IBC requires automatic sprinkler protection per NFPA 13 once the fire area crosses the code thresholds — which commercial-scale facilities routinely do. Idaho enforces IBC-based codes statewide"},
    {"question": "Are you licensed to seal drawings in Idaho?", "answer": "Yes. Apex Grid Engineering is licensed in 49 states — every U.S. state except Alaska — plus the District of Columbia, and every sealed sheet comes from responsible-charge engineering by a licensed PE, never a standalone stamp."},
    {"question": "Single-story or multi-story for a Idaho site?", "answer": "It depends on land cost and the submarket. Boise is one of the West's fastest-growing metros; in-migration from coastal states has outpaced storage supply in the Treasure Valley. Where land is affordable, single-story drive-up product builds fastest and cheapest; where land is dear, multi-story climate-controlled buildings stack the rentable square footage. We model both against the pro forma before the design is locked."}
    ],
