    {"question": "Do you design climate-controlled storage in Salt Lake City?", "answer": "Yes — it is the majority of what we design. semi-arid with real winters — Wasatch snow loads and freeze protection plus hot dry summers We size HVAC around 1,250 to 1,600 square feet per ton with wide setpoints for long runtimes that wring out moisture, and dedicated dehumidification where the climate demands it. The humidity target is engineered first, because humidity excursions are what generate tenant claims."},
    {"question": "What fire code requirements apply to storage in Salt Lake City?", "answer": "Self-storage is IBC Group S-1 moderate-hazard storage occupancy, which drives sprinkler requirements per NFPA 13, allowable heights and areas, and egress design. Utah enforces IBC-based statewide codes; seismic provisions apply along the Wasatch fault Compartmentalized units with limited access make the sprinkler and fire alarm design more demanding than the simple building form suggests — the fire protection has to be engineered for the actual unit layout, not just the shell."},
    {"question": "Can you handle a multi-site rollout across Utah?", "answer": "That is the core of the practice. We engineer the prototype once — unit mix logic, structural system, MEP approach — then produce jurisdiction-adapted drawing sets for each site: same design DNA, re-engineered for local wind, seismic, snow, energy code editions, and the AHJ's amendments. Utah frequently ranks #1 in state population growth rate — young demographics and large households feed storage demand One point of responsibility across every site, on a rolling two-week cadence."},
    {"question": "Are you licensed in Utah?", "answer": "Yes — Apex Grid Engineering is licensed in 49 states plus DC, and every sealed sheet comes from a licensed PE in responsible charge. For a developer building in Salt Lake City, that means the same accountable engineering team from the first site to the fifth, with no handoffs between firms."}
