    "answer": "Apex Grid Engineering designs self-storage facilities in Kenosha, Wisconsin — structural, mechanical, electrical, plumbing, and civil engineering for climate-controlled and drive-up projects, delivered on a two-week permit cycle for developers building at speed. Chicago-Milwaukee corridor city with logistics and commuter growth Milwaukee and Madison anchor demand; the Fox Valley (Appleton-Green Bay) adds manufacturing-driven growth One integrated team under one roof means no inter-discipline delays: the structural, MEP, and civil packages arrive coordinated, sealed by licensed PEs, and ready for plan check.",
    "sections": [
    {"heading": "Self-storage development in Kenosha", "body": "Kenosha, Wisconsin: Chicago-Milwaukee corridor city with logistics and commuter growth. Milwaukee and Madison anchor demand; the Fox Valley (Appleton-Green Bay) adds manufacturing-driven growth Dane County (Madison) posts the state's strongest population growth on tech and university expansion The development math that drives site selection — household formation, renter turnover, and corridor visibility — is exactly what draws new storage projects to this market even as national deliveries moderate. Madison-Milwaukee I-94 corridor Developers who win here move on a developer's schedule: entitled sites, compressed design phases, and permit drawings that keep pace with construction financing."},
    {"heading": "MEP and structural design for Kenosha storage projects", "body": "A Kenosha facility needs the full integrated package. Structurally, most projects use pre-engineered metal building (PEMB) systems or conventional steel with the long clear spans that make drive aisles work, engineered per ASCE 7 for the site's wind, seismic, and snow criteria. The MEP scope starts with climate: humid continental with severe winters — snow loads, freeze protection, and heating design dominate Climate-controlled buildings target roughly 55 to 85 degrees Fahrenheit with relative humidity held below 60 percent — the HVAC design uses wide-setpoint split systems sized around 1,250 to 1,600 square feet per ton with dedicated dehumidification, engineered for the humidity number first because that is where mold claims come from. Electrical covers LED site and unit lighting, gate operators, keypad access control, camera and security rough-in, and the leasing office; plumbing serves office restrooms and site hose bibbs, and fire alarm and sprinkler monitoring tie into the access system. Every discipline is sealed by a licensed PE under responsible charge."},
    {"heading": "Site planning in Kenosha: drive aisles, access, and stormwater", "body": "A storage facility's revenue is drawn in plan view before a single footing is poured, and the civil engineering has to hold the whole pro forma together. Unit mix — 5x5s through 10x30s, climate-controlled versus drive-up, plus RV and boat storage where the submarket supports it — must match local demand, and every unit has to be rentable: drive aisles wide enough for box trucks and trailer turning radii, one-way circulation where it reduces conflicts, gate and keypad placement that keeps queues off the street. Madison-Milwaukee I-94 corridor Fire access lanes, hydrant coverage, and the fire department's turning requirements constrain the layout as hard as any zoning setback, and stormwater detention has to satisfy the municipality without eating the rentable footprint — underground detention under drive aisles where land is tight. We engineer the civil, structural, and MEP packages together so the site plan that wins the pro forma is the site plan that passes plan check."},
    {"heading": "Permitting storage in Kenosha — and the two-week cycle", "body": "Self-storage classifies as IBC Group S-1 moderate-hazard storage occupancy under Section 311.2, which drives the fire protection strategy: automatic sprinkler protection per NFPA 13 once the fire area crosses code thresholds, with the design accounting for compartmentalized units and limited fire department access. Wisconsin Commercial Building Code (IBC-based, SPS 361-366) enforced statewide Because the S-1 classification, sprinkler triggers, and local amendments vary by AHJ, the permit package has to be coordinated across disciplines before submittal — structural, MEP, and civil drawn together, not reconciled after the first correction cycle. That coordination is what lets us deliver permit-ready drawings on a two-week cycle once the basis of design is set: no inter-discipline delays, no redesign loops, and a submittal the plan reviewer can move through. For developers doing multi-site rollouts, that tempo compounds — every site moves at the same speed."}
    ],
    "faqs": [
    {"question": "How fast can you engineer a storage project in Kenosha?", "answer": "Two weeks for permit-ready drawings once the basis of design is complete — site plan, unit mix, and building type confirmed. That turnaround exists because the structural, MEP, and civil packages are produced by one integrated team instead of three firms coordinating by email. Developers building across Wisconsin get the same two-week cycle on every site, which is what makes multi-site rollouts financeable on a tight schedule."},
    {"question": "What does self-storage engineering cost in Kenosha?", "answer": "Fees scale with building type and scope — single-story drive-up versus multi-story climate-controlled, ground-up versus conversion. Nationally, storage hard costs run roughly $50 to $120 per square foot, and engineering is a small fraction of that. The expensive risk is not the fee; it is uncoordinated disciplines causing redesign loops that push construction starts. One integrated team removes that risk, and the fee stays predictable across sites when the prototype carries forward."},
    {"question": "Do you design climate-controlled storage in Kenosha?", "answer": "Yes — it is the majority of what we design. humid continental with severe winters — snow loads, freeze protection, and heating design dominate We size HVAC around 1,250 to 1,600 square feet per ton with wide setpoints for long runtimes that wring out moisture, and dedicated dehumidification where the climate demands it. The humidity target is engineered first, because humidity excursions are what generate tenant claims."},
    {"question": "What fire code requirements apply to storage in Kenosha?", "answer": "Self-storage is IBC Group S-1 moderate-hazard storage occupancy, which drives sprinkler requirements per NFPA 13, allowable heights and areas, and egress design. Wisconsin Commercial Building Code (IBC-based, SPS 361-366) enforced statewide Compartmentalized units with limited access make the sprinkler and fire alarm design more demanding than the simple building form suggests — the fire protection has to be engineered for the actual unit layout, not just the shell."},
    {"question": "Can you handle a multi-site rollout across Wisconsin?", "answer": "That is the core of the practice. We engineer the prototype once — unit mix logic, structural system, MEP approach — then produce jurisdiction-adapted drawing sets for each site: same design DNA, re-engineered for local wind, seismic, snow, energy code editions, and the AHJ's amendments. Dane County (Madison) posts the state's strongest population growth on tech and university expansion One point of responsibility across every site, on a rolling two-week cadence."},
    {"question": "Are you licensed in Wisconsin?", "answer": "Yes — Apex Grid Engineering is licensed in 49 states plus DC, and every sealed sheet comes from a licensed PE in responsible charge. For a developer building in Kenosha, that means the same accountable engineering team from the first site to the fifth, with no handoffs between firms."}
    ],
