    {"heading": "MEP and structural design for Jackson storage projects", "body": "A Jackson facility needs the full integrated package. Structurally, most projects use pre-engineered metal building (PEMB) systems or conventional steel with the long clear spans that make drive aisles work, engineered per ASCE 7 for the site's wind, seismic, and snow criteria. The MEP scope starts with climate: semi-arid continental with extreme winters — severe snow loads and freeze protection Climate-controlled buildings target roughly 55 to 85 degrees Fahrenheit with relative humidity held below 60 percent — the HVAC design uses wide-setpoint split systems sized around 1,250 to 1,600 square feet per ton with dedicated dehumidification, engineered for the humidity number first because that is where mold claims come from. Electrical covers LED site and unit lighting, gate operators, keypad access control, camera and security rough-in, and the leasing office; plumbing serves office restrooms and site hose bibbs, and fire alarm and sprinkler monitoring tie into the access system. Every discipline is sealed by a licensed PE under responsible charge."},
    {"heading": "Site planning in Jackson: drive aisles, access, and stormwater", "body": "A storage facility's revenue is drawn in plan view before a single footing is poured, and the civil engineering has to hold the whole pro forma together. Unit mix — 5x5s through 10x30s, climate-controlled versus drive-up, plus RV and boat storage where the submarket supports it — must match local demand, and every unit has to be rentable: drive aisles wide enough for box trucks and trailer turning radii, one-way circulation where it reduces conflicts, gate and keypad placement that keeps queues off the street. Cheyenne-Front Range spillover corridor Fire access lanes, hydrant coverage, and the fire department's turning requirements constrain the layout as hard as any zoning setback, and stormwater detention has to satisfy the municipality without eating the rentable footprint — underground detention under drive aisles where land is tight. We engineer the civil, structural, and MEP packages together so the site plan that wins the pro forma is the site plan that passes plan check."},
    {"heading": "Permitting storage in Jackson — and the two-week cycle", "body": "Self-storage classifies as IBC Group S-1 moderate-hazard storage occupancy under Section 311.2, which drives the fire protection strategy: automatic sprinkler protection per NFPA 13 once the fire area crosses code thresholds, with the design accounting for compartmentalized units and limited fire department access. Wyoming has no statewide commercial building code; Cheyenne and Casper adopt the IBC locally Because the S-1 classification, sprinkler triggers, and local amendments vary by AHJ, the permit package has to be coordinated across disciplines before submittal — structural, MEP, and civil drawn together, not reconciled after the first correction cycle. That coordination is what lets us deliver permit-ready drawings on a two-week cycle once the basis of design is set: no inter-discipline delays, no redesign loops, and a submittal the plan reviewer can move through. For developers doing multi-site rollouts, that tempo compounds — every site moves at the same speed."}
